Planning skill optimization: a minimal dependency-aware cache
===============================================================

Decision
--------

Reduce resume-session rereads with a small generated cache experiment. The
canonical Markdown plan documents remain authoritative: plan-description.md,
work-unit-inventory.md, goal and step documents, working context, progress,
handoffs, reviews, and other existing planning artifacts. Existing document
IDs and helper conventions remain authoritative as well:

  plan
  goal:<goal>
  step:<goal>/<step>
  unit:<WNN>

The cache is derived guidance, never a second source of truth. Phase 1 does
not introduce a P/G/S/A identity namespace, facts, deltas, compaction, or
worker orchestration. Those ideas are deferred until measured use shows that
the cache does not solve the observed token and stale-context costs.

Phase 1 scope
-------------

Add a generated context directory and a minimal `plan-context.sh` command with
only `init`, `read`, `check`, and `refresh`. It must use the existing planning
parsers and document IDs rather than duplicate identity or paragraph rules.
All selectors and values use explicit long flags; positional numeric goal and
step indexes are not supported.

Plan initialization must also create a Git repository inside the plan
directory for local change tracking:

- `init` runs `git init` only when the plan directory has no `.git` directory;
- an existing plan-local repository is reused and never reinitialized;
- the plan's initial canonical artifacts receive an initial commit;
- `.git/` and Git metadata are excluded from cache dependencies and generated
  content; and
- initialization refuses to silently absorb unrelated files or pre-existing
  uncommitted changes.

The plan repository is a history boundary for plan artifacts, not a replacement
for the parent project's repository. The helper must detect and clearly report
when the plan directory is already inside another repository, because nested
repositories can confuse users and parent-repository status output.

Global plan identity and lookup index
-------------------------------------

Plan creation must allocate one globally incrementing plan ID from the
planning-skill registry before the plan is considered initialized. Use a fixed
format such as `P000001`; IDs are never reused, even if plan creation fails or
the plan is later deleted. Gaps are acceptable and preserve auditability.

The registry is a fixed-schema, lock-protected index at the planning skill's
plans root, for example:

  <planning-skill-dir>/plans/index.tsv

Each row records at least:

  plan-id | canonical-plan-path | title | status | created-at

The allocated ID must also be written into the plan's canonical metadata during
creation, using the existing document-generation/update helpers rather than a
hand-edited line. The plan ID is part of the initial plan-local Git commit.

The registry and plan metadata must agree before initialization succeeds. If
registry allocation succeeds but plan creation or its initial commit fails,
the ID remains reserved with a failed/repair-needed status; it must not be
silently returned to the pool. If the plan is created but registry update fails,
the helper must block normal use and report a repair-needed state rather than
creating a second ID or pretending registration succeeded.

All registry mutations require an exclusive registry lock and atomic rewrite.
The registry is not a substitute for plan-local Git history. The plan-local
repository tracks plan content; the global registry tracks identity and path.
Moving a plan directory requires a helper command that updates the canonical
path in the registry and creates a plan-local Git commit. Direct moves leave a
path mismatch that must be reported as a repair condition.

Lookups should accept the stable global ID:

  plan-context.sh read --plan-id P000001 --document goal:01-foundation
  plan-context.sh check --plan-id P000001
  plan-context.sh lookup --plan-id P000001

The helper resolves the ID through the registry and refuses ambiguous or
unregistered paths. A direct `--plan-dir` may remain available for bootstrap
and repair, but normal agent operations should use `--plan-id` so the registry
provides one canonical path. The plan ID must appear in compact output,
changelog entries, cache manifests, and Git commit trailers where applicable.

The generated snapshot contains fixed-schema files such as:

  context/snapshots/<generation>/index.tsv
  context/snapshots/<generation>/manifest.tsv
  context/snapshots/<generation>/entries/
  context/current

The index maps existing document IDs to current canonical paths. The manifest
records source dependencies and hashes for each generated entry, plus the
cache schema version and generator/parser version. Generated files are never
included in their own source hash scope.

Cache entries and dependencies
------------------------------

An entry is a bounded generated view identified by an existing document ID
and a fixed view name, for example `goal:01-foundation/resume` or
`unit:W07/acceptance`. Each entry records:

- entry ID and view name;
- every canonical source path used to produce it;
- the hash of every dependency at generation time;
- schema version, generator/parser version, and generation number.

Dependencies are explicit, not inferred from the entry's primary document.
A goal resume may depend on its goal document, plan description, inventory,
progress, owned steps, and required completed handoffs. A section read depends
on the canonical document containing that section. A changed source therefore
invalidates every entry that names it, using a reverse dependency map. The
check reports changed sources and affected entry IDs separately.

The processed scope is the set of cache entries with a manifest record. A
normal bounded `read` registers the selected entry and all dependencies used
by that view; `--read-only` does not register anything. `init` creates an empty
cache and reports “no processed dependencies,” not “the plan is globally
fresh.” Unprocessed sources do not make a routine check stale. This is a cost
optimization, not proof that the plan's unprocessed scope is unchanged.

Selectors and bounded reads
---------------------------

Use existing IDs directly:

  plan-context.sh read --plan-dir <dir> --document goal:01-foundation
  plan-context.sh read --plan-dir <dir> --unit W07
  plan-context.sh read --plan-dir <dir> --document step:01-foundation/02-verify \
    --section acceptance-criteria

`read` returns only the requested fixed view or section, with a default byte
and record limit. It reports the limit and a continuation token when output is
truncated. `--max-bytes` and `--max-records` may lower the defaults; an
explicit `--full` is not part of Phase 1. Direct canonical source reads remain
available through the existing helpers.

Generated reads fail with a machine-readable `stale` result when any recorded
dependency, index input, schema version, or generator version no longer
matches. They do not print stale guidance as normal content. A bounded
`--read-only` read may inspect canonical source content, but it never bypasses
staleness for generated content.

Freshness, changed scope, and refresh
-------------------------------------

Examples:

  plan-context.sh check --plan-dir <dir>
  plan-context.sh check --plan-dir <dir> --changed --format json
  plan-context.sh refresh --plan-dir <dir> --entry goal:01-foundation/resume
  plan-context.sh refresh --plan-dir <dir> --stale

`check` compares only processed entries and their complete dependency sets.
It also verifies the index manifest: a new, removed, renamed, or re-associated
canonical object makes the processed index scope stale. `--changed` is
diagnostic only; it emits the changed-source and affected-entry sets from that
single check and does not create state for a later command. `refresh --stale`
recomputes freshness and refreshes that exact set under one lock and snapshot.
Explicit `--entry` selectors refresh only those entries and their required
dependencies; they do not silently expand to unprocessed entries. A bounded
fanout summary reports sources changed, entries invalidated, entries rebuilt,
and whether output was truncated.

Exit categories are stable and machine-readable: `0 fresh`, `1 stale`,
`2 usage/selector error`, `3 invalid or missing cache`, `4 source missing or
deleted`, `5 lock contention`, and `6 write or internal failure`. JSON output
has a versioned fixed schema with arrays for changed sources, affected entries,
removed objects, and errors. Text output is compact and never expands full
paths or document content unless requested.

Renames, deletions, and identity
--------------------------------

Existing logical document, goal, step, and work-unit IDs identify objects;
paths are mutable attributes. A rename performed through the planning helpers
preserves the ID and invalidates entries using the old path. A genuine
deletion tombstones the ID in the index, invalidates dependent entries, and
never permits that ID to be reused. A path replacement is treated as a
deletion plus a new object unless the existing helper can prove the logical ID
was preserved. Phase 1 does not invent artifact IDs or aliases.

Consistency and locking
-----------------------

Every write builds and validates a complete new snapshot in a temporary
generation directory, then atomically switches `context/current` to it. The
helper takes an exclusive plan-context lock for refresh and for source-helper
cache invalidation; reads take a shared lock or open one immutable generation.
Check, dependency calculation, refresh, and snapshot publication use the same
generation, so `check` and `refresh --stale` cannot operate on different
marker sets. Lock contention returns category 5 without partial files.

Source mutations through existing planning helpers invalidate known dependent
entries in the same operation when possible. The manifest remains the recovery
mechanism for external edits, interrupted writes, and missed invalidations.

Helper-managed Git commits
---------------------------

Every mutating helper command must produce one plan-local Git commit after its
validated file changes. Read-only commands such as `read`, `check`, and status
inspection do not commit. A helper must stage only the exact files it changed;
it must never use broad `git add -A`, `git add .`, or commit unrelated files.

Commit messages must be generated from structured mutation data and contain the
logical target, operation, and affected work units or document IDs. Use a
searchable format such as:

  plan(01-foundation): update acceptance criteria [W07]

Include machine-searchable trailers when applicable:

  Plan-Document: goal:01-foundation
  Work-Units: W07,W08
  Change-Version: v9
  Change-Operation: update

The helper should provide compact directed-history commands or documented Git
equivalents, for example:

  git log --oneline --grep='W07'
  git log --oneline -- path/to/goal.md
  git show --stat <commit>

This allows an agent or human to identify what changed and where without
loading the complete history. The changelog/version lookup in Phase 1.5 and
Git history should reference the same document IDs and mutation version.

Commit handling is part of the helper transaction:

- source changes, version metadata, changelog, cache invalidation, and the
  commit are treated as one logical mutation;
- a commit is created only after formatting and validation pass;
- if Git identity, locking, staging, or commit creation fails, the helper must
  report failure and must not claim the mutation is complete or fresh;
- failed commits leave a bounded `commit-pending` state for human recovery and
  block subsequent plan mutations until the state is resolved; and
- helpers must never use `git reset --hard`, discard uncommitted work, rewrite
  history, or bypass hooks to force a commit.

An external uncommitted edit in the plan repository is a suspect condition. It
must be reported alongside hash/version mismatches and handled by the
Phase-1.5 human-review/quarantine rules rather than automatically staged or
committed by an agent.

Audit boundaries
----------------

Routine resume checks may use processed scope. A broader `check --all` audit is
required before declaring a goal complete, after scope/decomposition,
ownership, or dependency changes, after a source rename or deletion, after a
validator/helper/schema/parser change, and before a fresh adversarial review.
The audit reports unprocessed additions and changes but does not register or
refresh them automatically. Any audit finding that changes scope must be
reflected in the canonical documents and pass the existing planning review and
validation gates.

Phase 1.5: versioned source changes and compact changelogs
-----------------------------------------------------------

Add helper-managed version metadata after the Phase 1 cache behavior is stable.
Each canonical logical document keeps a monotonic version number, independent
of its filesystem path. A rename preserves the document ID and version
history; a genuine deletion tombstones the ID and prevents reuse.

Every mutation performed through an approved planning helper must update, in
one atomic operation:

- the canonical document version;
- a compact changelog entry;
- the affected cache dependency record; and
- the generated snapshot when the changed entry can be rebuilt safely.

The changelog belongs to the helper-owned document format or its canonical
document history record. It must not be hand-edited. Each entry records only
the information needed to explain the change:

  version | operation | document-id | target | summary

Operations are limited to `insert`, `update`, `remove`, `rename`, and
`external-change`. The target should identify a known section, paragraph, work
unit, or document field. Do not copy complete old and new paragraphs into the
default changelog; that would recreate the token bloat this design is intended
to prevent.

Provide a bounded change lookup:

  plan-context.sh changes --plan-dir <dir> \
    --document goal:01-foundation --from-version 6 --to-version 9

The default output is a compact summary of inserted, updated, removed, and
renamed targets. A separately requested diff mode may return the exact bounded
content change. The normal `read` command must not include changelog history
unless explicitly requested.

The cache manifest tracks both integrity and semantic change state:

  document-id | version | sha256 | cache-generation

Versions do not replace hashes. A matching version with a mismatching hash is
an external or corrupted edit and must be stale until reconciled. If a source
is edited outside a helper, the next check reports `external-change`; it must
not silently advance the version or declare the cache fresh. Treat this as a
suspect external-edit incident requiring human intervention. It may be a
legitimate human edit, but the system must assume that an agent attempted to
bypass the helper contract until a human confirms otherwise.

Rogue-agent handling
--------------------

On a version/hash mismatch, the helper must fail closed and create a compact,
append-only incident record containing:

- logical document ID and path;
- expected version and hash;
- observed hash and timestamp;
- affected cache entries and dependencies; and
- incident state `suspect-external-edit`.

The helper must preserve the prior manifest/snapshot and must not overwrite the
source, rewrite its changelog, increment its version, or rebuild its cache in
response to the mismatch. It may provide read-only diagnostic output, but it
must not emit the suspect content as trusted generated guidance.

While a suspect incident is open:

- source-mutating planning helpers reject mutations to the affected document;
- cache refresh, version reconciliation, and compaction for the affected
  document or dependent entries are rejected;
- `--force`, `--ignore-hash`, `--accept-current`, and equivalent bypass flags
  do not exist;
- repeated attempts are recorded as a bounded attempt count, not allowed to
  rewrite or clear the incident; and
- agent instructions say to stop retrying, stop trying alternate mutation
  commands, preserve the diagnostic output, and request a human decision.

Unrelated plan documents may continue only if their dependency graph is proven
not to include the quarantined object. A whole-plan write lock is acceptable if
dependency isolation cannot be established safely.

Clearing the incident requires an out-of-band human approval recognized by the
execution environment, not merely a flag or text argument that an agent can
invent. The approval workflow should expose two choices:

1. reject or restore the external edit from a trusted snapshot; or
2. accept it after review, assigning the next version, recording an
   `external-change-approved` changelog entry, and rebuilding affected cache
   entries in one atomic transaction.

If the environment cannot authenticate a human approval, the incident remains
open and the plan remains quarantined. Never automatically “repair” the file
just to make the hash pass.

Version-aware freshness allows a helper mutation to update the known version
and rebuilt cache entry together, making the affected entry fresh again without
requiring a broad plan reread. If the cache cannot be rebuilt in the same
operation, the version advances but the entry remains stale until an explicit
refresh.

Phase 1.5 acceptance criteria:

- helper mutations increment exactly one logical document version per changed
  document and never reuse a version;
- renames preserve document identity and version history;
- changelog entries identify inserted, updated, removed, and renamed targets;
- changelog output is bounded and excluded from default reads;
- `changes` supports an explicit version range and fixed compact output;
- the manifest records version and hash, and both must match for freshness;
- external edits create a suspect incident, preserve prior state, fail closed,
  block affected writes and refreshes, and require authenticated human review;
- retrying alternate mutation commands cannot clear or overwrite a suspect
  incident; and
- every approved mutating helper command creates one exact-scope, searchable
  plan-local Git commit, while read-only commands create none;
- failed Git commits leave a bounded `commit-pending` state and never cause the
  helper to discard, reset, or silently commit unrelated files; and
- version, changelog, manifest, and rebuilt snapshot updates are atomic or leave
  a recoverable invalid state.

Later phases, conditional on measurement
-----------------------------------------

Measure representative sessions: bounded read output, cache-generation cost,
refresh fanout, stale-read incidents, and total model-visible tokens compared
with the existing helper workflow. If Phase 1 does not provide a material
benefit, stop.

Only if evidence justifies it, consider append-only events in a later phase.
Events must have a version, ID, timestamp, kind, message, existing document or
work-unit relation, snapshot ID, provenance, and promotion status; they must
never become authoritative silently. Define retention and audit semantics
before considering compaction. Consider worker boundaries only after measuring
startup and rediscovery cost; compaction must never be an assumed continuation
operation for an active worker.

Acceptance criteria
-------------------

The Phase 1 proposal is implementation-ready when its implementation and
bounded tests demonstrate:

- canonical Markdown and existing helper/document IDs remain the sole source
  of truth, with no P/G/S/A namespace;
- plan creation allocates a globally unique, monotonically increasing `PNNNNNN`
  ID, records it in canonical metadata, and registers its canonical path in a
  locked fixed-schema global index;
- failed creation reserves its ID rather than reusing it, and registry/plan
  disagreement enters repair-needed state;
- every cached entry has explicit dependencies, per-dependency hashes, schema
  and generator versions, and a bounded output contract;
- normal reads register only the selected processed entry and dependencies;
  read-only reads do not, and empty processed scope is distinguished from
  globally fresh scope;
- stale generated reads fail clearly, while direct canonical reads remain
  available;
- `check`, diagnostic `--changed`, explicit refresh, and atomic
  `refresh --stale` have unambiguous semantics and stable error categories;
- index changes, renames, deletions, replacements, dependency fanout, and ID
  tombstones are detected without ID reuse;
- locking and generation snapshots prevent mixed cache/manifest/index states;
- helper-managed versions and bounded changelogs report source changes without
  broad rereads, while hashes still detect external edits;
- plan creation allocates a globally unique, never-reused plan ID, writes it
  into canonical plan metadata, and registers the ID with its canonical path
  under a locked global index;
- plan initialization creates a plan-local Git history boundary, and every
  helper mutation produces one exact-scope, searchable commit;
- output limits, truncation signaling, JSON schema, and fanout summaries are
  tested;
- required `--all` audit triggers are documented and exercised; and
- Phase 2 events, compaction, and workers remain explicitly deferred pending
  measurements.

No implementation is authorized by this proposal alone. The subsequent plan
must name the concrete helper, parser, validator, generated-file, and test
work units under the planning skill's atomic work-unit rules.
